    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An attic bedroom usually has more floor area than it feels like it has. The problem is not space, it is height: a sloped ceiling turns part of the floor into somewhere you can stand, part into somewhere you can sit, and part into somewhere only a box will fit. Get the furniture right and a loft room becomes one of the cosiest bedrooms in the house. Get it wrong and you spend every morning ducking.</p>
<p>The method below is the one we use on attic projects: measure the headroom first, decide what each height band is for, then choose furniture to suit. It works whether you have a full loft conversion or a single bedroom tucked under the eaves.</p>

<h2 id="map-your-headroom">Step 1: Map your headroom before you buy anything</h2>
<p>Floor plans lie in attic rooms, because they show area but not height. Before you choose any sloped ceiling bedroom furniture, make a simple height map.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Find the 2 m (6 ft 6 in) line.</strong> Stand a tape measure upright and walk it towards the eaves until the slope meets 2 m. Mark the floor with a strip of masking tape. Everything on the high side is full standing height for walking, dressing and opening wardrobe doors.</li>
<li><strong>Find the 1.5 m (5 ft) line.</strong> Repeat at 1.5 m and tape a second line. Between the two lines is the "stooping zone": ideal for anything you do sitting down, such as a desk or dressing table under a dormer, a reading chair or the head end of a bed, but not for walking through.</li>
<li><strong>Find the 1 m (3 ft 3 in) line.</strong> Below about 1 m is low storage territory only: drawers, baskets, books and anything you reach from a kneeling or sitting position.</li>
<li><strong>Note the knee wall height.</strong> Many attics have a short vertical wall where the slope stops, often somewhere between 60 cm and 1.2 m (2 ft and 4 ft). Measure it at several points, as older houses are rarely consistent.</li>
<li><strong>Mark windows, skylights, hatches, radiators and sockets.</strong> These fix where things can and cannot go far more than you might expect.</li>
</ul>
<p>Draw the room on squared paper with the three lines on it. You now have a plan that shows what the room can actually do.</p>

<h2 id="bed-under-the-slope">Step 2: Put the bed under the slope, headboard low</h2>
<p>The single most useful move in an attic bedroom is placing the bed so the head end sits under the slope and the foot end points into the full-height part of the room. You only need full height when you are standing, and you never stand at the head of the bed.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Check sitting-up height.</strong> Sit on the mattress where your pillows will go. You want roughly 90 cm–1 m (36–39 in) of clearance above the mattress so you can sit up to read without hitting your head. If you cannot get that, pull the bed a little further out from the knee wall.</li>
<li><strong>Use a low bed frame.</strong> A frame with a mattress top around 45–50 cm (18–20 in) from the floor buys you headroom compared with a tall divan or a deep ottoman base. Platform beds work well here.</li>
<li><strong>Skip the tall headboard.</strong> A low, padded headboard or a simple panel fixed to the knee wall is enough. Alternatively, run a slim shelf along the knee wall at pillow height to act as a headboard and bedside surface in one.</li>
<li><strong>Leave a side walkway.</strong> Keep at least 60 cm (24 in) at the side of the bed you get in from, measured in the area where you can stand or stoop comfortably. In a tight double room, it is fine to push one side against the wall if only one person sleeps there.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid sleeping right under a skylight</strong> if you can help it: it is draughty in winter, bright on summer mornings and noisy in rain. Offset the bed so the skylight sits over the floor in front of it.</li>
</ul>
<p>For general clearances around beds, see our <a href="/blog/bedroom-furniture-arrangement-guide">bedroom furniture arrangement guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="eaves-storage">Step 3: Turn the low zone into storage</h2>
<p>The space below 1 m (3 ft 3 in) is where attic rooms gain or lose their storage. Leaving it empty wastes it; filling it with standard furniture never quite fits.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Knee-wall drawers.</strong> Built-in drawers set into the knee wall are the neatest solution, using the void behind the wall as drawer depth. Drawers usually work better than cupboards here, because you can see everything without crawling in. Check with a builder or joiner what is behind the wall first: insulation, pipes, wiring or roof structure may limit depth.</li>
<li><strong>Low chests and sideboards.</strong> If built-ins are not an option, look for freestanding chests around 60–80 cm (24–31 in) high. Measure the slope at the back edge of the piece, not the front, because that is where it will touch.</li>
<li><strong>Under-eaves cupboards with sliding fronts.</strong> Hinged doors need swing space; sliding or lift-off panels do not. Use the deepest, lowest part for things you need a few times a year, such as suitcases and seasonal bedding.</li>
<li><strong>Underbed drawers.</strong> If your low bed frame allows it, rolling drawers make up for wardrobe space you cannot have elsewhere.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="clothes-storage">Step 4: Hang clothes only where height allows</h2>
<p>A standard hanging rail needs more height than most people assume. Long coats and dresses need around 1.6–1.8 m (5 ft 3 in–6 ft) of clear drop below the rail, and shirts and jackets around 1 m (3 ft 3 in) plus the rail and hanger.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Full-height wardrobes go on the gable wall</strong> or wherever the ceiling is above 2 m (6 ft 6 in). This is usually the end wall, not the long sloped side.</li>
<li><strong>Short-hanging rails can go in the stooping zone.</strong> Between the 1.5 m and 2 m lines, a rail set at around 1.3–1.4 m (4 ft 3 in–4 ft 7 in) takes shirts, skirts and folded trousers.</li>
<li><strong>Open rails beat bulky wardrobes</strong> when height varies. A freestanding clothes rail with a shelf above and baskets below can sit exactly where the height suits it, and moves if you change the layout later.</li>
<li><strong>Check door swing and drawer pull-out.</strong> Wardrobe doors need around 60 cm (24 in) clear in front of them, and drawers need their full depth plus space to stand. Plan these against the 2 m line, not the floor plan.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before ordering a wardrobe or chest, cut its silhouette out of cardboard at full size and hold it against the slope. It takes ten minutes and saves the frustration of a piece that is a few centimetres too tall at the back.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="lighting">Lighting a room with a sloped ceiling</h2>
<p>Sloped ceilings make central pendants awkward: there is often no flat ceiling to hang from, and a dropped shade can end up exactly where someone stands.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use the ridge or flat section.</strong> If there is a flat strip at the top, flush or semi-flush fittings work there. Keep anything hanging above head height in the walkway.</li>
<li><strong>Wall lights on the knee wall or gable.</strong> Plug-in or wired wall lights either side of the bed free up bedside surfaces, which are often shallow under a slope.</li>
<li><strong>Angled spotlights or track</strong> fitted to the slope can be pointed down at the floor, the wardrobe and the desk. Choose warm white bulbs and fittings designed for sloped mounting, and use a qualified electrician for anything wired, as insulation and ventilation in roof spaces need care.</li>
<li><strong>Low-level lamps</strong> under the eaves make the lowest part of the room glow and feel deliberate rather than dark.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/bedroom-lighting-and-curtains-guide">bedroom lighting and curtains guide</a> covers bulb colour and layering in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="colour-on-slopes">Colour on slopes and walls</h2>
<p>In attic rooms, the walls and ceiling run into each other, so the question is where colour stops.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Colour-drench it.</strong> Painting the walls, slopes and ceiling the same colour blurs the angles and is often the calmest option, especially in small or irregular rooms. A soft, warm mid-tone looks better than a stark white in rooms with little daylight.</li>
<li><strong>Light slopes, deeper knee walls.</strong> Painting the knee wall and gable a deeper shade and keeping the slope pale draws attention to the generous part of the room.</li>
<li><strong>Paint the slope behind the bed</strong> as a feature, so it acts as a headboard. This works well when the bed sits under the slope as described above.</li>
<li><strong>Use a matt or eggshell finish.</strong> Sloped ceilings catch light at raking angles and show every dip in the plaster; flatter finishes hide more.</li>
</ul>
<p>For colour choice, read our <a href="/blog/bedroom-colour-guide">bedroom colour guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="skylight-blinds">Skylight and roof window blinds</h2>
<p>Roof windows let in a lot of light and can make an attic bedroom hard to sleep in on summer mornings. Standard curtains do not work on a slope, because they hang away from the glass.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use blinds made for roof windows.</strong> These run in side channels that hold them against the glass at any angle. Blackout versions are the practical choice for a bedroom.</li>
<li><strong>Match the blind to the window model.</strong> Roof window blinds are usually sized to the window manufacturer's frame code, so check the label on the frame before ordering.</li>
<li><strong>Consider a pleated or duo blind</strong> on windows over a desk or seating area, where you want filtered light rather than blackout.</li>
<li><strong>Dormer windows</strong> can take ordinary curtains or a Roman blind, but keep the stack-back clear of the glass so daylight is not lost.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common attic bedroom mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Buying furniture from a floor plan without checking height at the back edge.</li>
<li>Putting the bed in the full-height area and wasting standing room.</li>
<li>Leaving the eaves empty, then running out of storage.</li>
<li>Hanging a pendant where people walk.</li>
<li>Blocking a roof window or the access route with tall furniture. Keep escape routes and windows clear, and check building and fire regulations if the room is a conversion.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="planning-your-attic-bedroom">Planning your attic bedroom</h2>
<p>Start with tape on the floor at 1 m, 1.5 m and 2 m, then give each height band one job: standing and wardrobes in the tallest part, sitting and the head of the bed in the middle, storage at the bottom. Once that is clear, the right furniture is much easier to choose.</p>
<p>For more ideas, read our guides to <a href="/blog/small-bedroom-decorating-and-storage-ideas">small bedroom decorating and storage</a> and <a href="/blog/bedroom-furniture-arrangement-guide">bedroom furniture arrangement</a>. Our <a href="/services/bedroom-decoration">bedroom decoration</a> service can plan furniture, storage and lighting around your slopes, or you can <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
